          "reasoning": "We need to write a single query (SELECT/WITH) that computes net total per client: sum of values of 'ok' orders minus all refunds for those orders. Cancelled orders and their refunds are excluded. Must preserve all clients, including those with no orders, with total zero. Totals can be negative. Must not double-count order value when multiple refunds exist (i.e., order value counted once, refunds summed). So net = sum(order.valor where estado='ok') - sum(reembolso.valor where reembolso.pedido_id references an ok order). Use LEFT JOINs and aggregate. Use COALESCE for null values.\n\nWe need to ensure that refunds for cancelled orders are excluded. So join refunds to orders where order.estado='ok'.\n\nWe also need to include clients with no orders: left join from clientes to derived aggregates.\n\nWe can use CTEs: orders_agg per client sum of order values (only ok). refunds_agg per client sum of refund values for ok orders. Then final select.\n\nSQL:\n\nWITH ok_orders AS (\n  SELECT cliente_id, SUM(COALESCE(valor,0)) AS sum_orders\n  FROM pedidos\n  WHERE estado='ok'\n  GROUP BY cliente_id\n),\nok_refunds AS (\n  SELECT p.cliente_id, SUM(COALESCE(r.valor,0)) AS sum_refunds\n  FROM reembolsos r\n  JOIN pedidos p ON r.pedido_id = p.pedido_id\n  WHERE p.estado='ok'\n  GROUP BY p.cliente_id\n)\nSELECT c.cliente_id,\n       c.nome,\n       COALESCE(o.sum_orders,0) - COALESCE(r.sum_refunds,0) AS total_liquido\nFROM clientes c\nLEFT JOIN ok_orders o ON c.cliente_id = o.cliente_id\nLEFT JOIN ok_refunds r ON c.cliente",
